Add Authentication Manually
Video 6 Script (same tone and structure as Video 1)

Today we are going to add authentication to this app.

Real authentication.
Login.
Session.
Protected route.

No lecture.
No theory.
Just wiring real infrastructure.

Here is what we are going to do.
We will install authentication.
We will protect a route.
And we will deploy.

That’s it.

The tools are the same as before.
GitHub.
Vercel.
Antigravity.
Supabase.

For this video, we’ll use Supabase Auth.
You could use NextAuth as well.
The principle is the same.

I assume you already have the project running.
Database connected.
Environment variables configured.
And deployments working.

My name is Steve.
I was a professional software tester for over 12 years.
My job was to verify that infrastructure behaves correctly under real conditions.
So today we wire this cleanly.
No shortcuts.

Let’s start.

First step.
Enable authentication in Supabase.

Go to your Supabase dashboard.
Go to Authentication.
Enable email login.

That’s it.
No custom server.
No password hashing logic.
Infrastructure handled.

Now we install the Supabase auth client in our app.
If it’s already installed, we just configure it.

We make sure our environment variables include the Supabase URL and anon key.
Locally and in Vercel.

Next step.
Create a simple login page.

One email field.
One password field.
One submit button.

We use the Supabase client to sign in with email and password.
No styling perfection.
Just function.

Now we add a protected route.

For example, /dashboard.

Inside that page, we check if a session exists.
If no session, redirect to login.
If session exists, render the page.

That’s it.
That is route protection.

We test locally.

Open the dashboard without logging in.
You should be redirected.

Now log in.
Go back to dashboard.
Now it loads.

This is not a demo anymore.
This is infrastructure.

Now we commit.

Clear message.
Add Supabase authentication and protect dashboard route.

We push to GitHub.

Vercel detects the change.
Triggers a build.
Redeploys.

Now we test production.

Open the live site.
Try to access the protected route directly.
You should be blocked.

Log in.
Access it again.
It works.

That’s it.

You just wired authentication manually.

No black box.
No magic SaaS platform.
You connected real infrastructure.

You now have database.
API routes.
Authentication.
Deployment loop.

This is how real apps are built.

In the next video, we’ll look at what happens when authentication goes wrong and how to prevent common mistakes before they hit users.

See you there.